[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Custom Directives in Tailwind CSS: @layer, @apply, @config, and @theme"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Tailwind CSS provides a robust set of custom directives that allow developers to extend, organize, and configure the framework directly within their CSS files. Understanding how to leverage @layer, @apply, @config, and @theme is crucial for scaling large applications and maintaining clean, maintainable design systems."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Organizing Styles with @layer"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The @layer directive tells Tailwind which stack a set of custom styles belongs to. Tailwind divides styles into three main layers: base, components, and utilities. Using @layer ensures that your custom styles respect specificity rules and tree-shaking correctly."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"@tailwind base;
@tailwind components;
@tailwind utilities;

@layer base {
  h1 {
    @apply text-2xl font-bold;
  }
}

@layer components {
  .btn-primary {
    @apply bg-blue-500 text-white px-4 py-2 rounded-lg;
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Reusing Utility Patterns with @apply"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The @apply directive allows you to inline any existing utility classes into your custom CSS rules. This is particularly useful for repetitive component styles where writing long class strings in HTML becomes impractical."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":".card {
  @apply bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl shadow-md p-6 max-w-sm mx-auto;
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Scoping Configurations with @config"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Tailwind CSS v4 and modern setups, the @config directive allows you to specify a different configuration file for a particular CSS entry point. This is extremely helpful in multi-tenant applications or when different parts of a project require isolated design tokens."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"@config "./tailwind.dashboard.config.js";

@tailwind base;
@tailwind components;
@tailwind utilities;"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Defining Design Tokens with @theme"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The @theme directive lets you define or override your design system variables directly in your CSS file without needing a JavaScript configuration file. You can set custom colors, spacing, fonts, and breakpoints seamlessly."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"@theme {
  --color-brand-primary: #3b82f6;
  --color-brand-secondary: #10b981;
  --font-display: "Inter", sans-serif;
  --spacing-container: 1200px;
}"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@layer: Controls where custom CSS rules are injected into the final stylesheet."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@apply: Extracts utility classes into custom CSS classes for cleaner templates."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@config: Points specific stylesheets to isolated Tailwind configuration files."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@theme: Injects custom design tokens directly into the CSS build pipeline."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"By mastering @layer, @apply, @config, and @theme, you can build scalable, highly customizable design systems with Tailwind CSS while keeping your codebase modular and maintainable."}]
